  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user's request: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final detailed technical tutorial. Need ensure complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls.\n\nNeed think structure. Could be tutorial with sections. Need maybe use PostgreSQL/MySQL examples. Need explain SQL execution pipeline: client sends query, protocol, authentication, parse (lexer/parser, AST), semantic analysis (catalog, types, permissions), rewrite (views, rules), planning (logical plan, cost model, statistics, index selection, join order, access paths), optimization (predicate pushdown, join reordering, subquery flattening, common subexpression), execution (Volcano/iterator, vectorized, pipelined), storage engine, MVCC, locks, transactions, isolation levels, concurrency, returning rows (fetch, buffering, streaming, network). Indexing: B-tree, hash, GIN/GiST, covering, partial, expression, composite, index-only scans, index condition pushdown, statistics, maintenance costs. Joins: nested loop, hash, merge, sort-merge, hash join, bloom filters, join order, equi vs non-equi, self joins, lateral, subqueries. Transactions: ACID, MVCC, locks, isolation levels, deadlocks, write skew, phantom, snapshot, 2PC, prepared transactions, savepoints. Performance pitfalls: missing indexes, bad selectivity, index bloat, stale stats, N+1, OR conditions, functions on indexed columns, implicit type casts, large IN lists, unbounded result sets, SELECT *, excessive joins, correlated subqueries, locking, long transactions, autocommit, connection pool, caching, query plan cache, parameter sniffing, partitioning, sharding.\n\nNeed avoid repeating. Could include examples:\nCREATE TABLE customers (id bigint primary key, region text, email text, created_at timestamptz);\nCREATE TABLE orders (id bigint primary key, customer_id bigint references customers(id), status text, amount numeric, placed_at timestamptz);\nCREATE INDEX orders_customer_status_idx ON orders(customer_id, status);\nQuery: SELECT c.email, o.amount FROM customers c JOIN orders o",
